        <div class="two-column" style="margin-top: 40px;">
            <div class="column">
                <div class="article-title">"What Do Deaconesses Do Anyway?"</div>
                <div class="author-info">By <span data-field="editorial_author_1">{{ editorial_author_1 }}</span></div>
                <div class="body-text">
                    <p>"What do deaconesses do anyway?" This is a question that I hear a lot these days as a student in the deaconess program. Finding an adequate yet succinct answer, however, has proven to be quite difficult. All what do deaconesses do?</p>
                    <p>During my journey through this program, I have learned that the answer is far more extensive than I even knew existed. It is a ministry of service, a ministry of listening, a ministry of presence, a ministry of compassion. It is a ministry of being there, of showing up, of caring, of listening. It is a ministry of love. It is a ministry of Christ's love.</p>
                    <p>Deaconesses are individuals called by the Holy Spirit and set apart by the church to do the work of diakonia—service. Diakonia is a Greek word that means service. It is the work of serving others. It is the work of serving God through serving others. It is the work of serving the poor, the sick, the lonely, the grieving, the hurting. It is the work of serving the community.</p>
                    <p>Diakonia is not performed for fear of punish- ment, nor is it performed for the promise of reward. It is performed out of love. It is performed because we are called to love. It is performed because we are called to serve. It is performed because we are called to be like Christ.</p>
                    <p>Deaconesses are those who are called to be like Christ in service. They are called to be the hands and feet of Christ in the world. They are called to be the compassionate presence of Christ in the world. They are called to be the listening ear of Christ in the world. They are called to be the comforting touch of Christ in the world.</p>
                    <p>The role of a deaconess is not to be a replacement for the pastor. The pastor is the shepherd, the one who guides the flock. The deaconess is the servant, the one who tends to the sheep. The pastor preaches the Word, the deaconess serves the Word. The pastor leads the congregation, the deaconess ministers to the individuals within the congregation.</p>
                    <p>Deaconesses are called to be the embodiment of Christ's love in action. They are called to be the visible sign of God's grace in the world. They are called to be the living embodiment of the Gospel.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="column">
                <div class="article-title">Serving Christ by Serving Our Neighbor: Theological and Historical Perspectives on Lutheran Deaconesses</div>
                <div class="author-info">By <span data-field="article_author_2">{{ article_author_2 }}</span> and <span data-field="article_author_3">{{ article_author_3 }}</span></div>
                <div class="body-text">
                    <p>Lutheran deaconesses serve Christ by serv- ing their neighbor. They receive their office from the Word of God, and they give his life as a ransom for many (Matthew 20:28).</p>
                    <p>The deaconess is a ministry of service, a ministry of listening, a ministry of presence, a ministry of compassion. It is a ministry of being there, of showing up, of caring, of listening. It is a ministry of love. It is a ministry of Christ's love.</p>
                    <p>Deaconesses are individuals called by the Holy Spirit and set apart by the church to do the work of diakonia—service. Diakonia is a Greek word that means service. It is the work of serving others. It is the work of serving God through serving others. It is the work of serving the poor, the sick, the lonely, the grieving, the hurting. It is the work of serving the community.</p>
                    <p>Deaconesses are those who are called to be like Christ in service. They are called to be the hands and feet of Christ in the world. They are called to be the compassionate presence of Christ in the world. They are called to be the listening ear of Christ in the world. They are called to be the comforting touch of Christ in the world.</p>
                    <p>The role of a deaconess is not to be a replacement for the pastor. The pastor is the shepherd, the one who guides the flock. The deaconess is the servant, the one who tends to the sheep. The pastor preaches the Word, the deaconess serves the Word. The pastor leads the congregation, the deaconess ministers to the individuals within the congregation.</p>
                    <p>Deaconesses are called to be the embodiment of Christ's love in action. They are called to be the visible sign of God's grace in the world. They are called to be the living embodiment of the Gospel.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
