    <br><br>
    <h2 style="text-align: center;">Deaconesses: Engaging the Church in Diakonia</h2>
    <br><br>
    The MISSION of Christ’s Body is to proclaim
    the Gospel in Word and provide the Gospel
    in Sacraments. But Christ’s Body doesn’t stop
    there. What is a corporate response of
    the Church to the Gospel? Diakonia. The Church
    cannot be the Church without diakonia.
    <br><br>
    Diakonia is mercy; diakonia is love directed
    towards suffering and need. When Christ’s
    Body shows mercy, it in fact serves Christ
    (Matthew 25:31-46).
    <br><br>
    A founder of the modern deaconess
    movement, Rev. Wilhelm Lohe, under-
    stood social service as essential mission
    and ministry of the church, belonging to
    every member and “entrusted to some—dea-
    conesses and deaconesses”—on behalf of the
    many.” The Church entrusts the diaconate
    to serve as a reminder for diakonia. The dia-
    conate places not only it on the Church’s
    mission but also on the Church’s mission.
    <br><br>
    Deaconesses serve where there is shame,
    hurt, pain, injustice, bigotry, violence, abuse—
    where people are on the margins. However,
    the deaconess has no corner on the market of
    diakonia. Instead, the deaconess keeps asking
    church leaders, “How can we approach
    diakonia?” Fill in the blank: music, youth,
    education, outreach, missions, assimilation,
    volunteer activities, etc. The deaconate also
    encourages the lady to view their vocations as
    vehicles for diakonia.
    <br><br>
    Diaconal care comes full circle when it
    points the sufferer to the true Physician. Lohe
